          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I am 52 and my LDL cholesterol is 165 mg/dL. Does that number alone decide treatment, what risk bands does guidance use, and which modifiable coronary-risk factors does the corpus describe?",
          "answer": "An LDL cholesterol level alone does not typically determine treatment decisions. Current guidance emphasizes that treatment is driven by an estimated 10-year atherosclerotic cardiovascular risk, rather than just the LDL number (guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation).\n\nRegarding risk stratification bands, the central approach is to assess a person's estimated 10-year atherosclerotic cardiovascular risk (guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation). For example, drug treatment for Stage 1 hypertension is considered if someone has an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk at or above 10 percent, alongside other conditions (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment). The retrieved passages do not provide a comprehensive list of all specific numerical risk stratification bands used by authorities.\n\nSeveral modifiable coronary-risk factors are mentioned in the guidelines:\n*   Reducing dietary sodium (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment)\n*   Adopting a dietary pattern rich in vegetables, fruit, whole grains and low-fat dairy, and low in saturated fat (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment)\n*   Regular aerobic activity most days of the week (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment)\n*   Weight reduction if body weight is above the healthy range (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment)\n*   Limiting alcohol (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment)\n*   Stopping smoking (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment)\n\nYou should discuss these factors and your overall cardiovascular risk with a clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
